var e=["Developer","General","Games"],t={name:"Jay J",tagline:"Senior Software Engineer & Architect",intro:"I build practical web tools, playable games and write about software development \u2014 all from one workshop.",about:["I am a full-stack developer with over a decade of experience designing, building and scaling web, mobile and SaaS applications across the .NET, JavaScript and Python ecosystems. Most of my work lives in the messy middle of real products \u2014 decomposing monoliths into microservices, designing REST APIs, tuning databases and shipping to AWS.","Lately I have been leading the migration of a payments-heavy SaaS platform to a reusable, Django-based microservices architecture adopted across a product suite \u2014 the kind of work where the architecture decisions matter more than any single feature. I also lead and mentor engineers, and enjoy taking a product from requirement analysis all the way to production.","This site is my workshop: a place to build small, useful browser tools and playable games, and to write about the software-architecture ideas behind them. Everything here runs entirely in your browser."],quickFacts:[{label:"Based in",value:"Tamil Nadu, India"},{label:"Focus",value:"Angular \xB7 TypeScript"},{label:"Open to",value:"Opportunities & collabs"}],skills:[{group:"Core",items:["Angular","TypeScript","RxJS","JavaScript (ES2023)"]},{group:"Frontend",items:["HTML5","SCSS / CSS","Responsive design","Accessibility"]},{group:"Tooling & practice",items:["Git & GitHub","REST APIs","Vite / Angular CLI","CI/CD (GitHub Actions)"]}],board:[{index:"01",label:"Portfolio",status:"live"},{index:"02",label:"Dev & general tools",status:"live"},{index:"03",label:"Chess & games",status:"live"},{index:"04",label:"Writing",status:"live"}],projects:[{title:"Gold Rate Today",description:"Live 22K / 24K gold prices per gram and per sovereign, fetched client-side and refreshed daily. First tool shipping on this site.",tags:["Angular","REST API","PWA-ready"],status:"building"},{title:"Chess",description:"Playable chess in the browser with legal-move validation and a simple computer opponent. Part of the games section.",tags:["TypeScript","chess.js","Canvas / DOM"],status:"queued"},{title:"Your past project",description:"Swap this card for real work experience or a project you have already shipped \u2014 what it does, the stack, and your role.",tags:["Angular","Your stack here"],status:"live",link:"https://example.com"}],tools:[{slug:"gold-rate",title:"Gold Rate Today",description:"Live 22K / 24K gold prices per gram and per sovereign, from the international spot price.",category:"General",icon:"\u{1FA99}",status:"live"},{slug:"jwt",title:"JWT Encode / Decode",description:"Decode a JSON Web Token to inspect its header and payload, or build and sign one (HS256).",category:"Developer",icon:"\u{1F510}",status:"live"},{slug:"chess",title:"Chess",description:"Play chess in the browser \u2014 full rules with check/checkmate and a simple computer opponent.",category:"Games",icon:"\u265F\uFE0F",status:"live"},{slug:"rolling-ball",title:"Rolling Ball Maze",description:"Roll a ball through a 3D maze with the arrow keys \u2014 dodge the pits and reach the goal. Built with Babylon.js.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F3B1}",status:"live"},{slug:"number-catch",title:"Number Catch",description:"A kids 3D game \u2014 move the basket to catch the right numbers (even, odd, multiples) and learn counting.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F522}",status:"live"},{slug:"shape-sorter",title:"Shape Sorter 3D",description:"A kids 3D game \u2014 steer each falling shape into its matching bin (cube, sphere, cone, pyramid).",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F537}",status:"live"},{slug:"math-maze",title:"Math Maze",description:"A kids 3D game \u2014 roll forward and steer into the lane with the correct answer to each sum.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F9EE}",status:"live"},{slug:"alphabet-hunt",title:"Alphabet Hunt",description:"A kids 3D game \u2014 tap the floating letters in alphabetical order (A to H) to learn the alphabet.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F524}",status:"live"},{slug:"color-match",title:"Color Match Sorter",description:"A kids 3D game \u2014 catch only the falling balls that match the target colour, and learn colour names.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F308}",status:"live"},{slug:"memory-match",title:"Memory Match 3D",description:"A kids 3D game \u2014 flip tiles to find matching pairs of animals and fruits in as few moves as you can.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F0CF}",status:"live"},{slug:"animal-sound",title:"Animal Sound Match",description:"A kids 3D game \u2014 see an animal and tap the sound it makes; the game says each sound out loud.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F436}",status:"live"},{slug:"feed-animal",title:"Feed the Animal",description:"A kids 3D game \u2014 tap the right food for each animal (meat or plants) to learn what they eat.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F37D}\uFE0F",status:"live"},{slug:"pattern-completer",title:"Pattern Completer",description:"A kids 3D game \u2014 spot the repeating colour pattern and tap the colour that comes next.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F9E9}",status:"live"},{slug:"catch-objects",title:"Catch the Falling Objects",description:"An arcade 3D game \u2014 move the basket to catch falling fruit and dodge the bombs.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F9FA}",status:"live"},{slug:"whack-a-mole",title:"Whack-a-Mole 3D",description:"An arcade 3D game \u2014 tap the moles as they pop out of their holes, against a 30-second clock.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F528}",status:"live"},{slug:"endless-runner",title:"Endless Runner",description:"An arcade 3D game \u2014 auto-run forward, switch lanes and jump to dodge obstacles. Beat your best.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F3C3}",status:"live"},{slug:"target-shooting",title:"Target Shooting Gallery",description:"An arcade 3D game \u2014 shoot moving targets against the clock, with particle hits and accuracy tracking.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F3AF}",status:"live"},{slug:"platformer",title:"Basic Platformer",description:"A 3D platformer \u2014 run and jump across platforms to reach the flag without falling.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F3C1}",status:"live"},{slug:"racing",title:"Mini Racing",description:"A 3D racing game \u2014 accelerate, brake and steer around a track for 3 timed laps.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F3CE}\uFE0F",status:"live"},{slug:"tower-stack",title:"Tower Stack",description:"An arcade 3D game \u2014 time your drop to stack sliding blocks; the overhang gets sliced away.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F9F1}",status:"live"},{slug:"tic-tac-toe",title:"3D Tic-Tac-Toe",description:"The classic game on a 3D board, with a minimax computer opponent that plays perfectly on Hard.",category:"Games",icon:"\u2B55",status:"live"},{slug:"connect-dots",title:"Connect All Dots",description:"A one-line puzzle \u2014 drag through every dot exactly once without crossing your path. Endless generated levels.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F535}",status:"live"},{slug:"jigsaw",title:"Jigsaw Puzzle",description:"Drag and snap pieces to rebuild the picture \u2014 10 categories, adjustable difficulty, or upload your own photo.",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F9E9}",status:"live"},{slug:"word-builder",title:"Word Builder",description:"A kids 3D game \u2014 tap letter blocks in order to spell the pictured word (cat, dog, star\u2026).",category:"Games",icon:"\u{1F520}",status:"live"},{slug:"image-editor",title:"Image Editor",description:"Upload an image, convert to grayscale or black & white, crop it, and download \u2014 all in your browser.",category:"General",icon:"\u{1F5BC}\uFE0F",status:"live"},{slug:"doc-scanner",title:"Document Scanner",description:"Turn a phone photo of a document into a clean black & white scan with adaptive thresholding.",category:"General",icon:"\u{1F5A8}\uFE0F",status:"live"},{slug:"date-calculator",title:"Date Calculator",description:"Calculate an age, the difference between two dates, or add/subtract days to find a date \u2014 with tabs.",category:"General",icon:"\u{1F4C5}",status:"live"},{slug:"base64",title:"Base64 Encode / Decode",description:"Convert text to Base64 and back, with UTF-8 support and a URL-safe option.",category:"Developer",icon:"\u{1F524}",status:"live"},{slug:"json-formatter",title:"JSON Formatter",description:"Pretty-print, minify and validate JSON, with clear error messages when it is invalid.",category:"Developer",icon:"\u{1F9FE}",status:"live"},{slug:"uuid",title:"UUID Generator",description:"Generate random v4 UUIDs in bulk, with uppercase and no-hyphen options.",category:"Developer",icon:"\u{1F194}",status:"live"},{slug:"hash",title:"Hash Generator",description:"Compute SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-384 and SHA-512 hashes of any text via the Web Crypto API.",category:"Developer",icon:"#\uFE0F\u20E3",status:"live"},{slug:"password",title:"Password Generator",description:"Create strong random passwords with a length slider, character options and a strength meter.",category:"Developer",icon:"\u{1F511}",status:"live"},{slug:"text-bytes",title:"Text \u2194 Bytes",description:"Convert text to its UTF-8 byte values in decimal, hex or binary \u2014 and back to text.",category:"Developer",icon:"\u{1F522}",status:"live"},{slug:"text-counter",title:"Text Counter",description:"Count characters, words and lines, and see the exact byte size of your text as you type.",category:"General",icon:"\u{1F4CF}",status:"live"}],links:{github:"https://github.com/jayaraj-jay",linkedin:"https://linkedin.com/in/your-profile",email:"trytocoder@gmail.com"},footerNote:"Built with Angular \xB7 Deployed on GitHub Pages"},a={role:"Senior Software Engineer & Architect",experienceYears:"10+",email:"jaycuddalore@gmail.com",location:"Chennai, India",summary:"Full-Stack Developer with 10+ years of experience designing, building and scaling web, mobile and SaaS applications across the .NET, JavaScript and Python ecosystems. Proven expertise in microservices architecture, REST API development, database design and performance tuning, and cloud deployment on AWS. Currently driving the migration of a cloud POS platform (RAVZORA) to a reusable Django-based microservices architecture adopted across the organization's product suite. Experienced in leading teams, mentoring developers and delivering products end to end \u2014 from requirement analysis to production.",skills:[{group:"Languages",items:["JavaScript","TypeScript","C#","Python"]},{group:"Backend",items:["Django",".NET Core / MVC","Node.js","Java Spring Boot"]},{group:"Frontend",items:["Angular","ReactJS","Ionic"]},{group:"Databases",items:["PostgreSQL","MySQL","MSSQL","Cassandra"]},{group:"Cloud & DevOps",items:["AWS (Lambda, S3, API Gateway, ELB)","CI/CD","Git","Jira"]},{group:"Architecture",items:["Microservices","REST APIs","SaaS multi-product platforms"]}],experience:[{role:"Senior Software Engineer",company:"Helixbeat Pvt Ltd, Chennai",period:"Jul 2025 \u2013 Present",note:"Joined Helixbeat following its acquisition of the ALLPOS product from Allmark Device Integrations; continuing as a core engineer on the same platform, rebranded as RAVZORA.",points:["Key contributor to RAVZORA (formerly ALLPOS Cloud POS), the flagship cloud-based Point of Sale SaaS platform.","Designing and implementing a shared microservices architecture in Python and Django, built as reusable services to power the entire product suite.","Decomposing monolithic modules into independent, scalable microservices \u2014 improving modularity, maintainability and deployment flexibility.","Developing REST APIs and integrating them with Angular front-end applications across products.","Performing database design, query tuning and performance optimization on PostgreSQL, and mentoring the team on microservices best practices."]},{role:"Senior Software Engineer",company:"Allmark Device Integrations Pvt. Ltd., Chennai",period:"Nov 2018 \u2013 Jul 2025",points:["Led development of scalable web applications using Angular and ReactJS, ensuring best practices and high performance.","Built and integrated .NET Core REST APIs with front-end applications, collaborating closely with backend teams.","Implemented microservices architecture to enhance application modularity and maintainability.","Managed CI/CD pipelines, streamlining deployments and reducing release cycles.","Mentored junior developers through code reviews and technical guidance; optimized application performance and load times."]},{role:"Software Engineer",company:"Gritsys Technologies, Chennai",period:"Aug 2015 \u2013 Oct 2018",points:["Designed and developed responsive web pages compatible across multiple devices and browsers.","Built dynamic front-end components for web and mobile applications using .NET MVC5, Ionic and Angular.","Developed cross-platform mobile applications with Ionic, optimizing performance and consistency across iOS and Android.","Leveraged Razor views in .NET MVC5 to integrate server-side rendering with front-end components."]}],projects:[{name:"RAVZORA \u2014 Cloud POS (Microservices Migration)",tech:"Python \xB7 Django \xB7 PostgreSQL \xB7 Angular \xB7 AWS",detail:"Re-architecting the cloud POS platform into Django-based microservices designed for reuse across all company products \u2014 service design, API development, database architecture, and AWS deployment with automated S3 backups."},{name:"Allpos \u2014 Cloud POS (SaaS)",tech:".NET Core \xB7 MySQL \xB7 Angular \xB7 Android Native",detail:"Requirement gathering and system design; developed REST APIs and MySQL procedures, a service-based database layer, query tuning and automated daily backups to AWS S3."},{name:"ALLPOS \u2014 HRMS",tech:"ReactJS \xB7 Flutter \xB7 MySQL \xB7 .NET Core",detail:"Designed a microservice-based architecture supporting modular development, scalability and rapid iteration; built the front end in ReactJS."},{name:"Supplybuy \u2014 eCommerce Platform",tech:"Python \xB7 Java Spring Boot \xB7 Cassandra \xB7 Flutter \xB7 AWS",detail:"Led a young team; built backend services with Python and Java Spring Boot, implemented AWS API Gateway and load-balancer integrations, and designed the Cassandra architecture with automated S3 backups."},{name:"Warehouse Management System",tech:"Angular \xB7 .NET Core \xB7 MySQL \xB7 Java \xB7 Flutter",detail:"Requirement analysis, database design and architecture; developed REST APIs and MySQL stored procedures with query tuning and performance optimization."},{name:"FineAck \u2014 Accounts Receivable Follow-up",tech:"Ionic + Angular \xB7 MySQL \xB7 .NET Core",detail:"Developed a hybrid mobile app for accounts-receivable follow-up; built REST APIs, database design, stored procedures and automated backups to AWS S3."}],education:[{degree:"Bachelor of Information Technology",period:"Aug 2008 \u2013 Jun 2012"}]},n=[{topic:"Microservices",icon:"\u{1F9E9}",items:[{q:"How do you decide where to draw service boundaries?",a:"Follow the natural seams where data ownership and rate of change diverge \u2014 bill something because billing rules changed, not because catalogue did. Aim boundaries at capabilities a single team can own end to end, and avoid drawing one around a noun the whole company argues about, because everyone will keep reaching across it."},{q:"What is a \u201Cdistributed monolith\u201D and how do you avoid it?",a:"It is a set of services that deploy separately but cannot change or fail independently, usually because they share a database or must be updated together in one transaction. You avoid it by giving each service its own private data and a versioned API, and by designing cross-service workflows for eventual consistency instead of shared transactions."},{q:"How do you migrate a monolith to microservices safely?",a:"Incrementally, using the strangler-fig pattern: put a routing layer in front, extract one capability at a time into a new service, and redirect that slice of traffic behind a flag so you can roll back in minutes. Split the data carefully \u2014 dual-write, backfill, reconcile, then cut over \u2014 because splitting the data is harder and riskier than splitting the code."},{q:"How do services stay consistent without shared transactions?",a:"Through eventual consistency and patterns like the saga: each service does its local work and publishes an event, and compensating actions undo earlier steps if a later one fails. Consumers must be idempotent so replayed events do no harm, and you accept a short window where the system is converging rather than perfectly in sync."}]},{topic:"REST API Design",icon:"\u{1F50C}",items:[{q:"What makes an API well designed?",a:"Consistency above all \u2014 resources named as nouns, HTTP verbs carrying intent, and the same shape for pagination and errors on every endpoint. The endpoints that hurt in production are the inconsistent ones that force every consumer to special-case your quirks."},{q:"Which HTTP status codes matter and why?",a:"200/201 for success, 400 for a bad request the client can fix, 401/403 for auth versus permission, 404 for a missing resource, 409 for a conflict, 422 for validation, 429 for rate limiting, and 500/503 for server-side failures. The distinction that matters most to clients is whether retrying could help \u2014 a 503 might, a 400 never will."},{q:"How do you paginate large collections?",a:"Cursor-based pagination with an opaque token pointing at a stable sort key. Offset pagination looks fine on page one but degrades in performance and correctness at deep pages, especially when rows shift underneath you between requests."},{q:"How should an API handle versioning?",a:"Treat additive changes as safe and breaking changes as rare and deliberate. Never quietly change the meaning of a field; when you must break compatibility, ship a new version and keep the old one alive long enough for consumers to migrate on their own schedule."}]},{topic:"Databases & Data",icon:"\u{1F5C4}\uFE0F",items:[{q:"When would you choose SQL versus NoSQL?",a:"Reach for SQL when the data is relational and you need strong consistency, ad-hoc queries and transactions \u2014 most business systems. Reach for NoSQL when you have a known access pattern at very high scale, a flexible or document-shaped schema, or write volumes a single relational node cannot take. The honest default is a relational database until a specific requirement forces otherwise."},{q:"What is an N+1 query problem?",a:"It is when fetching a list issues one query for the list and then one extra query per item \u2014 a hundred rows becoming a hundred and one round trips. You fix it by eager-loading the related data in a single join or batched query (select_related / prefetch in Django, Include in EF), which ORMs make easy to trigger accidentally."},{q:"How do database indexes work and what is the cost?",a:"An index is a sorted structure (usually a B-tree) that lets the engine find rows without scanning the whole table, turning a linear lookup into a logarithmic one. The cost is slower writes and extra storage, since every insert or update must also maintain the index \u2014 so you index the columns you filter and join on, not every column."},{q:"What do the ACID properties guarantee?",a:"Atomicity (a transaction fully happens or not at all), Consistency (it moves the database from one valid state to another), Isolation (concurrent transactions do not corrupt each other), and Durability (a committed result survives a crash). They are why relational databases are the safe default for anything involving money or critical state."}]},{topic:"Caching & Performance",icon:"\u26A1",items:[{q:"Explain the cache-aside pattern.",a:"The application checks the cache first; on a miss it loads from the source, stores the result, and returns it. It is simple and resilient \u2014 if the cache is unavailable you just pay the slow path \u2014 and it is the workhorse for read-heavy data."},{q:"How do you keep a cache from serving stale data?",a:"Pick an invalidation strategy per data type based on how bad staleness is: expire via TTL for data that tolerates a small delay, delete-on-write when it must be fresh, or update-on-write when you can afford the extra moving parts. There is no single right TTL \u2014 only the data\u2019s tolerance for being slightly out of date."},{q:"What is a cache stampede and how do you prevent it?",a:"It is when a popular key expires and many requests miss simultaneously and all hit the database at once. Prevent it with a lock or single-flight refresh so only one request rebuilds the value, and by adding jitter to TTLs so keys do not all expire together."},{q:"Where should caching live?",a:"It depends on the trade-off you need: in-process is fastest but not shared, a distributed cache like Redis is shared and consistent across instances at the cost of a network hop, and a CDN caches at the edge for static or read-heavy content. Real systems usually combine layers rather than pick one."}]},{topic:"Messaging & Events",icon:"\u{1F4E8}",items:[{q:"When do you use events instead of a direct call?",a:"Use request/response when the caller needs the answer to continue; use events when the producer does not care who consumes or what they do. Events let new consumers plug in without touching the producer and stop a slow consumer from slowing the producer down \u2014 at the cost of eventual consistency and harder debugging."},{q:"What does \u201Cat-least-once\u201D delivery imply for consumers?",a:"It means a message can be delivered more than once, so consumers must be idempotent \u2014 processing the same message twice must have the same effect as processing it once. You typically dedupe on a message id or design the handler so re-processing is naturally safe."},{q:"What is a dead-letter queue?",a:"It is where messages go after repeatedly failing to process, so they stop blocking the queue and can be inspected or replayed later. Without one, a single poison message can stall an entire consumer."}]},{topic:"System Design & Reliability",icon:"\u{1F3D7}\uFE0F",items:[{q:"How do you make retries safe?",a:"Make the operation idempotent (or protect creates with an idempotency key), retry only errors that might succeed next time, and back off exponentially with jitter and a cap. A naive retry loop can turn a brief hiccup into an outage by hammering a recovering service."},{q:"What problem does a circuit breaker solve?",a:"It stops a service from repeatedly calling a dependency that is already failing. After a threshold of failures it \u201Copens\u201D and fails fast for a while, giving the struggling dependency room to recover and preventing the failure from cascading back up the call chain."},{q:"How would you scale a read-heavy system?",a:"Cache aggressively, add read replicas to spread query load off the primary, and push static or read-heavy content to a CDN. If writes become the bottleneck you then look at partitioning or sharding, but read scaling is usually the cheaper first win."},{q:"What is the difference between horizontal and vertical scaling?",a:"Vertical scaling means a bigger machine \u2014 simple, but bounded by the largest box and a single point of failure. Horizontal scaling means more machines behind a load balancer \u2014 more resilient and effectively unbounded, but it requires the app to be stateless (or to externalise state) so any instance can serve any request."}]}],i=[{slug:"reading-an-explain-plan",title:"Reading an EXPLAIN plan: debugging a slow Postgres query",date:"2026-08-26",readingTime:"6 min read",excerpt:"When a query is slow, guessing is expensive. EXPLAIN ANALYZE tells you exactly what the database is doing \u2014 once you learn to read it, most slow queries have an obvious fix.",tags:["Databases","PostgreSQL","Performance"],content:["The first instinct when a query is slow is to start changing things \u2014 add an index here, rewrite a join there \u2014 and hope. It is faster, and far less frustrating, to ask the database what it is actually doing. In Postgres that means EXPLAIN, and better still EXPLAIN ANALYZE, which runs the query and reports the real plan with real timings.","The plan is a tree you read from the innermost node outward: each node is an operation (a scan, a join, a sort) with an estimated cost, an estimated row count, and \u2014 with ANALYZE \u2014 the actual rows and time. The single most useful habit is comparing the estimated rows to the actual rows. When they diverge wildly, the planner is working from bad statistics and choosing a bad strategy; a plain ANALYZE on the table to refresh its stats often fixes the plan on its own.","The classic red flag is a Sequential Scan on a large table where you expected an Index Scan. Postgres reads every row because either there is no usable index, or the query hid the column behind a function or a type cast so the index cannot apply \u2014 filtering on lower(email) will not use a plain index on email. The fix is an index that matches how you actually query: a functional index, a composite index in the right column order, or a partial index for a common filter.","Joins tell their own story. A Nested Loop is efficient when the outer side is small, and catastrophic when it is large and the inner side is re-scanned millions of times \u2014 usually a sign of a missing index on the join key. A Hash Join or Merge Join over huge inputs, or a Sort that spills to disk, points at a query pulling far more data than it needs. Often the real fix is upstream: select fewer columns, filter earlier, or avoid the N+1 pattern that turned one query into a thousand.","None of this requires memorising the planner. The workflow is simple and repeatable: run EXPLAIN ANALYZE, find the node that dominates the time, check whether its row estimate matches reality, and ask why it is doing what it is doing. Nine times out of ten the answer is a missing or unusable index, stale statistics, or a query asking for more than it should \u2014 and each of those has a boring, reliable fix."]},{slug:"auth-across-services",title:"Authentication and authorization across services",date:"2026-08-22",readingTime:"6 min read",excerpt:"Authentication and authorization get lumped together and both get harder once you have many services. Keeping the two ideas \u2014 and their homes \u2014 separate is what keeps it manageable.",tags:["Architecture","Security","APIs"],content:['Authentication answers "who are you", authorization answers "are you allowed to do this". They are constantly conflated, and in a single application you can get away with it. Across many services you cannot: the two concerns want to live in different places, and mixing them creates either a security hole or a bottleneck.',"Authentication belongs at the edge. A user signs in once, and the system issues a signed token \u2014 commonly a JWT \u2014 that carries verified claims about who they are. The gateway or the auth service validates credentials; every service behind it just verifies the token\u2019s signature and trusts the claims inside. That is the whole point of a signed token: a service can confirm the identity without a round trip back to a central auth server on every request.","Authorization belongs close to the resource. Whether a user can edit this document or refund that order depends on data and rules the owning service knows best, so it should make that decision itself using the claims and roles in the token plus its own context. Pushing every authorization check into one central service turns it into a bottleneck and a single point of failure, and couples every team to its release schedule.","The token design is where teams get burned. Tokens should be short-lived, because a leaked long-lived token is a long-lived problem; pair a short access token with a longer refresh token so sessions survive without the risk. Keep the payload lean \u2014 an id and a few roles, not a snapshot of the whole user \u2014 because anything you embed is stale the moment something changes, and revocation of a stateless token is genuinely hard. Sometimes the honest answer is a short expiry plus a small deny-list for the rare forced logout.","The rule I keep coming back to: authenticate once, at the edge, and let services trust the verified result; authorize everywhere, close to the data being protected. Keeping those two responsibilities in their proper homes is what stops security from becoming either a single choke point or a hundred inconsistent checks."]},{slug:"requirements-to-production",title:"From requirements to production: taking a feature end to end",date:"2026-08-18",readingTime:"6 min read",excerpt:"Writing the code is often the easy part. The work that decides whether a feature actually lands is the thinking before it and the care around it.",tags:["Engineering","Process","Leadership"],content:["After enough years you notice that the hard part of shipping a feature is rarely the code. It is understanding what is actually being asked, fitting it into a system that already exists, and getting it into production without waking anyone at night. The typing in the middle is the part juniors worry about and seniors barely think about.",'It starts with the requirement behind the request. A stakeholder asks for a button; what they need is a workflow. Before designing anything I try to restate the problem back to whoever asked, in terms of the outcome they want, and I keep asking "what happens when" \u2014 when the input is empty, when the network drops, when two people do this at once. Most rework comes from building the literal request instead of the real need.',"Then comes the seam: where does this fit in the architecture? A good feature slots into existing boundaries and reuses what is there; a feature that forces a new coupling between services, or reaches across a boundary it should not, is a design smell worth resolving before writing code. This is where a little time spent on the shape of the change saves weeks of untangling later.","Delivery is a series of small, safe steps, not one big reveal. I break the work into increments that each ship on their own, put anything risky behind a feature flag, and lean on tests and observability so I can see the feature behaving in production rather than hoping it does. A flag also means the rollback is a config change, not an emergency deploy \u2014 which is what makes shipping calm instead of tense.","The last part is the least technical and the most underrated: bringing people along. Reviewing the design with the team, writing down the why, and mentoring whoever picks it up next is what turns a feature you built into a feature the team owns. Taking something from a vague requirement all the way to a boring, well-understood production deploy \u2014 and leaving the codebase and the team a little better for it \u2014 is the actual job."]},{slug:"event-driven-vs-request-response",title:"Event-driven or request/response? Choosing how services talk",date:"2026-08-06",readingTime:"6 min read",excerpt:"Synchronous calls are simple until they chain into a fragile tower. Events decouple, but move the complexity somewhere else. The choice is about coupling, not fashion.",tags:["Architecture","Messaging","Microservices"],content:["When two services need to interact you have two broad options: one calls the other and waits for an answer (request/response), or one announces that something happened and moves on, letting anyone interested react (event-driven). Teams often pick based on what is familiar rather than what the interaction actually needs, and then spend months fighting the consequences.","Request/response is the right default when the caller genuinely needs the answer to continue \u2014 a checkout has to know the payment succeeded before it confirms the order. It is easy to reason about and easy to debug, because the call stack tells the whole story. The danger is chaining: when service A calls B, which calls C, which calls D, you have built a synchronous tower where the slowest or least reliable link decides the fate of the whole request.",'Event-driven communication shines when the producer does not care who consumes, or what they do. An "order placed" event can fan out to inventory, analytics, email and loyalty without the order service knowing any of them exist. New consumers plug in without touching the producer, and a slow consumer cannot slow down the producer. That decoupling is the whole point.','The cost is that you trade a clear call stack for eventual consistency and harder debugging. There is no single trace that explains "why did this happen"; the truth is spread across a stream of events and the handlers that reacted to them. You need idempotent consumers, a dead-letter path for messages that keep failing, and good tracing, or the system becomes a set of things that mysteriously happen.',"In practice most real systems are both. Use request/response for the synchronous spine where a user is waiting on an answer, and events for the ripples that follow \u2014 the notifications, projections and side effects that do not need to block the response. The skill is knowing which parts of a workflow truly need an answer now, and which just need to happen eventually."]},{slug:"caching-strategies",title:"Caching that actually helps \u2014 and the kind that bites",date:"2026-08-04",readingTime:"6 min read",excerpt:"A cache is a second copy of the truth, and every copy can be wrong. Used well it is the cheapest performance win you have; used carelessly it serves stale data with confidence.",tags:["Architecture","Performance","Caching"],content:["Caching is the first tool people reach for when something is slow, and often the right one \u2014 reading from memory instead of recomputing or hitting a database is orders of magnitude faster. But a cache is fundamentally a second copy of data, and the moment you have two copies you have a consistency problem. Most caching pain is really invalidation pain in disguise.","The workhorse pattern is cache-aside: the application checks the cache, and on a miss it loads from the source, stores the result, and returns it. It is simple and resilient \u2014 if the cache is down, you just pay the slow path. The subtlety is expiry. A short TTL keeps data fresh but caches less; a long TTL caches more but risks serving stale reads. There is no universally right number, only the tolerance of that particular piece of data for being slightly out of date.","The hardest question is invalidation on write. When the underlying data changes, the cached copy is now a lie. You can expire it (let the TTL clean it up, accepting a window of staleness), delete it on write (simple, but a race can repopulate a stale value), or update it on write (fresh, but more moving parts). Picking one per data type \u2014 based on how bad stale data actually is \u2014 beats a blanket policy.","Know where the cache lives, because each layer has different trade-offs. An in-process cache is fastest but is not shared, so every instance has its own copy and its own staleness. A distributed cache like Redis is shared and consistent across instances, at the cost of a network hop. A CDN caches at the edge, brilliant for static and read-heavy content but blunt for anything personalised. The right answer is usually a combination, not a single layer.","Two failure modes are worth designing against up front. A cache stampede happens when a popular key expires and a thousand requests all miss and hit the database at once \u2014 a lock or a single refresh, or a slightly randomised TTL, prevents the thundering herd. And never let the cache become load-bearing for correctness: if losing the cache corrupts data rather than just slowing things down, it is no longer a cache, it is an undocumented primary store."]},{slug:"api-gateway-bff",title:"API gateway and BFF: giving each client the API it deserves",date:"2026-08-02",readingTime:"5 min read",excerpt:"One generic API for web, mobile and partners pleases none of them. A gateway centralises the cross-cutting concerns; a backend-for-frontend tailors the payload to each client.",tags:["Architecture","API","Frontend"],content:["As a system grows into many services, exposing all of them directly to clients becomes untenable: each client has to know where every service lives, and every service has to re-implement authentication, rate limiting and logging. An API gateway solves the first problem by giving clients a single entry point and handling the cross-cutting concerns once, in front of everything.","A gateway is the natural home for the things every request needs regardless of destination \u2014 terminating TLS, verifying tokens, enforcing rate limits, adding trace headers, and routing to the right service. Centralising these means a service can trust that a request which reached it is already authenticated and within quota, instead of re-checking on its own. The risk is letting the gateway grow business logic; it should route and protect, not decide.","The backend-for-frontend pattern addresses a different problem: a generic API forces every client to over-fetch and stitch data together. A mobile app on a slow connection wants a small, pre-composed payload in one round trip; a web dashboard wants richer data; a partner integration wants something stable and conservative. A BFF is a thin service dedicated to one client type, calling the underlying services and shaping exactly the response that client needs.","The win is that each frontend team owns its BFF and can move fast without waiting on a shared API committee, and without bloating the core services with client-specific fields. The cost is more moving parts and some duplicated aggregation logic across BFFs. It pays off when your clients genuinely differ; if web and mobile want nearly the same thing, a single well-designed API is simpler and you do not need the extra layer.","A useful way to hold the two ideas together: the gateway is about protection and routing for everyone, the BFF is about presentation for someone. Reach for a gateway when you have many services and many clients; add a BFF when a specific client is being poorly served by the one-size-fits-all API. Adding either before you feel that pain is usually just extra infrastructure to operate."]},{slug:"idempotency-and-retries",title:"Idempotency and retries: making \u201Ctry again\u201D safe",date:"2026-07-31",readingTime:"5 min read",excerpt:"In a distributed system, requests fail halfway and clients retry \u2014 so \u201Ccharge the card\u201D can run twice. Idempotency is what turns a dangerous retry into a harmless one.",tags:["Architecture","Reliability","APIs"],content:["The uncomfortable truth of distributed systems is that you can never be sure whether a request that timed out actually failed or merely lost its reply on the way back. The client, seeing no response, retries. If that request had a side effect \u2014 charging a card, creating an order, sending money \u2014 the retry can perform it a second time. This is not an edge case; at scale it happens constantly.",'Idempotency means an operation can run any number of times and leave the system in the same state as running it once. Reads are naturally idempotent; so is setting a value. The dangerous operations are the ones that increment, append or create \u2014 "add 100 to the balance" run twice is a bug, while "set the balance to 500" run twice is fine. Designing operations toward the latter shape removes whole classes of double-processing.',"When the operation is inherently a create, the standard tool is an idempotency key: the client generates a unique key for the attempt and sends it with the request. The server records the key alongside the result of the first successful execution. If the same key arrives again \u2014 the retry \u2014 the server recognises it, skips the work, and returns the original result. The client gets a consistent answer and the card is charged exactly once.","Retries themselves need discipline, because a naive retry loop can turn a brief hiccup into an outage. Retry only errors that might succeed next time (timeouts, 503s), never ones that will not (a 400 will always be a 400). Back off exponentially and add jitter so a thousand clients do not retry in lockstep and hammer a recovering service. And cap the attempts \u2014 infinite retries just move the failure around.","Put together, the pattern is: make operations idempotent where you can, protect the ones you cannot with idempotency keys, and retry carefully with backoff. It is a small amount of upfront design that converts the scariest property of distributed systems \u2014 that any call might silently happen twice \u2014 into a non-event."]},{slug:"django-dotnet-services",title:"Building services in Django and .NET Core: what each gets right",date:"2026-07-26",readingTime:"7 min read",excerpt:"Having built backends in both, I have come to see them as two answers to the same questions. Neither is \u201Cbetter\u201D \u2014 they optimise for different things, and knowing which is which makes you effective in both.",tags:["Django",".NET","Backend"],content:["Django and .NET Core solve the same problem \u2014 turning HTTP requests into database work and back \u2014 but they come from different philosophies. Django optimises for speed of delivery and convention; .NET Core optimises for explicitness, type safety and raw performance. Having shipped services in both, I have stopped asking which is better and started asking which fits the team and the workload.","Django\u2019s superpower is how little code stands between an idea and a running feature. The ORM, admin, migrations and auth come in the box and assume sensible defaults, so a small team can go from model to CRUD API in an afternoon. The trade is that the magic can hide cost \u2014 an innocent template or serializer can trigger a wall of N+1 queries \u2014 so you lean on tools like select_related and query logging to keep the convenience honest under load.",".NET Core makes you say more, and gives you more back for saying it. Static typing catches a class of mistakes at compile time that Python only finds at runtime, dependency injection is a first-class part of the framework rather than a library you add, and the async model and runtime deliver excellent throughput for I/O-heavy services. The cost is ceremony: more interfaces, more wiring, more upfront structure before the first endpoint responds.","The mental models rhyme more than they differ. Django\u2019s middleware is .NET\u2019s middleware pipeline; DRF serializers are model binding and validation attributes; Django\u2019s ORM and Entity Framework are both change-tracking abstractions over SQL that reward you for understanding the queries they generate. Once you see the correspondence, moving between them is mostly learning where each framework put the same idea.","How I choose in practice: Django when the goal is to validate a product quickly with a small team and a conventional data model; .NET Core when the service is performance-sensitive, long-lived, or large enough that compile-time guarantees and explicit structure pay for themselves. And in a microservices world you do not have to choose globally \u2014 each service can use the stack that suits it, as long as they agree on the contracts between them."]},{slug:"monolith-to-microservices",title:"Breaking a monolith into microservices without breaking production",date:"2026-08-05",readingTime:"7 min read",excerpt:"Decomposing a live, payments-heavy SaaS platform taught me that the hard part is not writing services \u2014 it is drawing the seams and cutting them one at a time while the business keeps trading.",tags:["Architecture","Microservices","Migration"],content:["The mistake most teams make with a monolith is treating the rewrite as a big-bang event: freeze features, spend six months rebuilding, then flip a switch and pray. In a product that is actively taking payments every second, that plan is a non-starter. The migration has to happen underneath live traffic, and every step has to be individually shippable and reversible.",'The first job is not code, it is boundaries. I look for seams where data ownership and rate of change already diverge \u2014 billing changes for different reasons than catalogue, which changes for different reasons than reporting. Those natural fault lines become service boundaries. A boundary drawn around a noun the whole company argues about (the classic "Order" or "Product") is a boundary you will regret, because every team keeps reaching across it.',"I use the strangler-fig pattern to cut, not a rewrite. A thin routing layer sits in front of the monolith; one capability at a time is extracted into a new service, and the router quietly redirects that slice of traffic. The monolith shrinks gradually while never going dark. If the new service misbehaves, you route back in minutes \u2014 the old code is still there.",'The part people underestimate is data. Two services cannot share a database table and still be independent; that shared table is just a monolith with extra network hops. Splitting the data is slower and scarier than splitting the code: you dual-write during the transition, backfill history, reconcile, and only then cut the old path. Getting this wrong is how "microservices" quietly become a distributed monolith that fails together.','What made it work in practice was ruthless observability and small steps. Every extraction shipped behind a flag, with metrics comparing old and new responses before the switch. The goal each week was not "the architecture is done" \u2014 it was "one more capability moved, nothing on fire". Architecture migration is a series of safe, boring deploys, and boring is exactly what you want when real money is moving through the system.']},{slug:"reusable-microservices-saas",title:"Designing microservices you can actually reuse across products",date:"2026-07-28",readingTime:"6 min read",excerpt:"A shared service that every product bends to fit becomes a bottleneck. Here is how I think about building services that are genuinely reusable across a multi-product platform.",tags:["Architecture","Microservices","SaaS"],content:["When an organisation runs several products, the promise of a shared microservices layer is obvious: build authentication, billing, notifications and inventory once, then reuse them everywhere. The trap is just as obvious in hindsight \u2014 a service owned by everyone is owned by no one, and every product that needs a small tweak pushes its own special case into the shared code until the service is a museum of exceptions.",'The rule I hold to is that a reusable service exposes capabilities, not product workflows. It should answer "charge this account" or "reserve this stock", not "run product X\u2019s checkout". The moment a shared service knows which product is calling and behaves differently, it has coupled itself to that product\u2019s roadmap and stopped being reusable. Product-specific orchestration belongs in the product, composed from generic building blocks.',"Contracts matter more than implementation. A reusable service lives behind a stable, versioned API, and that API is the actual product it ships. I treat breaking changes to it the way a library author treats a major version bump: additive by default, deprecate loudly, and never quietly change the meaning of a field. Consumers should be able to upgrade on their own schedule, not because the platform team forced a flag day.","Configuration is how you keep it generic without forking. Tax rules, currencies, notification templates and feature toggles are data the service is told about, not branches in its code. When a new product onboards, it configures the service; it does not modify it. If onboarding a product requires a code change in a shared service, the boundary is in the wrong place.","Reuse is a payoff you earn later, not a shortcut you take early. The first product that uses a service is really just validating the boundary. It is the second and third products \u2014 the ones you did not design for \u2014 that prove whether the abstraction was real or wishful. Keeping the surface small and the contract honest is what lets those later products plug in instead of pry it open."]},{slug:"rest-api-design-that-scales",title:"REST API design that survives real traffic",date:"2026-07-20",readingTime:"6 min read",excerpt:"Most API pain is designed in on day one. A few consistent choices \u2014 around resources, pagination, errors and versioning \u2014 save you from the rewrites later.",tags:["Architecture","REST APIs","Backend"],content:["An API is a contract you will be living with for years, usually long after you remember why you shaped it that way. The endpoints that hurt in production are rarely the slow ones \u2014 they are the inconsistent ones, where every consumer has to special-case your quirks. Consistency is the single highest-leverage design decision you make.","Model resources, not actions. A URL should name a thing \u2014 /orders, /orders/42/items \u2014 and the HTTP verb should carry the intent. When you find yourself designing /createOrder and /updateOrderStatus, you are tunnelling RPC through HTTP and throwing away everything the protocol gives you for free: caching, idempotency semantics, and a mental model every client already understands.","Design for large collections from the start, because every collection eventually gets large. Offset pagination looks fine on page one and falls apart at page ten thousand, both in performance and in correctness when rows shift underneath you. Cursor-based pagination \u2014 an opaque token pointing at a stable sort key \u2014 costs a little more to build and saves you a migration under load later.","Errors are part of the API, not an afterthought. A consumer needs a stable machine-readable error code, a human-readable message, and enough context to act, returned in a consistent shape on every failure. Leaking a database exception or a stack trace is both a security problem and a support problem; the client cannot branch on prose that changes every release.","Version before you need to, and make backwards-incompatible change the rare, deliberate act. Adding a field is safe; removing one, renaming one, or changing its meaning is not. If you cannot avoid a breaking change, ship it behind a new version and keep the old one alive long enough for consumers to migrate. The cost of a version is small; the cost of silently breaking every integration is not."]},{slug:"database-per-service",title:"Database-per-service: the boundary that actually matters",date:"2026-07-12",readingTime:"5 min read",excerpt:"You can split your code into a dozen services and still have a monolith if they all share one database. The real boundary in microservices is the data.",tags:["Architecture","Databases","Microservices"],content:["It is tempting, when moving to microservices, to keep one central database and just point all the new services at it. It feels efficient \u2014 one place for the data, joins still work, no duplication. It is also the fastest way to build a distributed monolith: services that deploy separately but cannot change, scale or fail independently, because they are all wired into the same schema.","The point of a service owning its own database is that the schema becomes private. No other service reads those tables directly, so you are free to change them \u2014 add a column, split a table, switch the storage engine \u2014 without coordinating a release across five teams. The database stops being a shared global variable and becomes an implementation detail hidden behind the service\u2019s API.","The obvious objection is joins. If orders and customers live in different databases, how do you query across them? The honest answer is that you often should not: you fetch from each service and compose in the application, or you keep a read-optimised copy of the data you frequently need. It feels like a step back until the first time you change one schema without breaking the other, and the trade becomes clear.","Cross-service consistency is the genuinely hard part, and pretending otherwise is how teams get burned. Without a shared transaction you cannot atomically update two services, so you design for eventual consistency \u2014 publish an event when something changes, let interested services react, and make those reactions idempotent so a replayed message does no harm. It is more work than a foreign key, but it is the price of independence.","My rule of thumb: if two services must always be updated together in one transaction, they are probably one service that has been split too finely. The boundary you draw around the data is the boundary that decides whether your system is actually made of independent parts \u2014 or just a monolith wearing a microservices costume."]},{slug:"client-side-chess-engine",title:"Building a chess engine in the browser, from scratch",date:"2026-07-02",readingTime:"6 min read",excerpt:"How the chess game on this site validates every move, detects check and checkmate, and plays back \u2014 all in a few hundred lines of dependency-free TypeScript.",tags:["TypeScript","Chess","Algorithms"],content:["When I set out to add a chess game to this site, the tempting shortcut was to reach for a library. Instead I wrote the whole engine by hand \u2014 board representation, move generation, and a small AI \u2014 because the point of a workshop is to actually build the thing.","The board is a flat array of 64 squares. Every piece knows its type and colour, and move generation walks each piece outward: sliding pieces (rook, bishop, queen) stop at the first blocker, while knights and kings use fixed offset tables. Pawns get special handling for the double-step, en passant, and promotion.",'The trick to legal moves is separating "pseudo-legal" from "legal". First I generate every move a piece could physically make, then I play each one on a copy of the board and discard it if it leaves my own king in check. That single filter gives you check, checkmate and stalemate detection almost for free.',"The opponent is classic minimax with alpha-beta pruning and a simple material-plus-position evaluation. It is not going to beat a serious player, but it punishes hanging pieces and makes the game fun \u2014 and it runs instantly in your browser with zero network calls."]},{slug:"everything-runs-in-your-browser",title:"Why every tool here runs 100% in your browser",date:"2026-06-24",readingTime:"4 min read",excerpt:"No servers, no uploads, no tracking. A look at the browser APIs that make it possible to ship real tools as a static site.",tags:["Web Platform","Privacy","Angular"],content:["Every tool on this site \u2014 the JWT signer, the hash generator, the image editor \u2014 runs entirely on your machine. Nothing you type or upload ever leaves the browser. That is a deliberate choice, and modern web APIs make it surprisingly easy.","The Web Crypto API handles hashing and HMAC signing natively, so the JWT and hash tools never need a backend. The Canvas API does all the pixel work for the image editor, including grayscale conversion and cropping. FileReader loads images locally, and crypto.getRandomValues powers the password generator with proper randomness.","The upside is real: there is nothing to hack, no data to leak, and the whole site deploys as static files. The cost is that heavy work happens on the client \u2014 but for these tools that is a rounding error, and the privacy guarantee is worth far more."]},{slug:"angular-signals-patterns",title:"Angular signals: the patterns I keep reaching for",date:"2026-06-15",readingTime:"5 min read",excerpt:"Derived state with computed(), local component state without a store, and why I rarely miss RxJS for UI logic anymore.",tags:["Angular","Signals","Architecture"],content:["This whole site is built on Angular signals, and after shipping a dozen tools with them a few patterns have become second nature.","The first is derived state. Instead of recomputing things in the template or juggling subscriptions, I lean on computed(). The text counter is a good example: one signal holds the raw text, and a single computed() derives characters, words, lines and byte size together. It only recalculates when the input changes.","The second is treating signals as plain local component state. For most interactive UI \u2014 a selected chess square, an active tab, a loading flag \u2014 a signal is all you need. No store, no boilerplate, and change detection stays fast because Angular knows exactly what changed.","I still reach for RxJS when there is genuine asynchrony to coordinate, like combining two HTTP calls for the gold-rate tool. But for the everyday work of reacting to user input, signals have made the code shorter and much easier to read."]}];export{e as a,t as b,a as c,n as d,i as e};
